      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Let me analyze this fragment of an OIML certificate.\n\nKey facts:\n- Certificate number: R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01, revision 14\n- Project number: 4009997\n- Page 1 of 13 → page_total = 13\n- Member State: The Netherlands\n- Scheme: A\n- Issuing authority: NMi Certin B.V., OIML Issuing Authority NL1\n- Person responsible: M.Ph.D. Schmidt\n- Address: Thijsseweg 11, 2629 JA Delft, The Netherlands\n- Phone: +31 88 636 2332\n- Email: certin@nmi.nl\n- Website: www.nmi.nl\n- Date issued: 8 January 2026\n- oiml_issuer_id: NL1\n\nApplicant and Manufacturer: ABB Limited, Oldends Lane, GL10 3TA Stonehouse, United Kingdom\n\nCertified type: electromagnetic water meter, Type: AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4 *\n- X being 1 for standard or 3 for advanced or 5 for premium (mobile Comms - MC) variant\n- Y being 1, 2, 8 or 9 (integral/remote version, optionally sensor only)\n- Z being R (reduced bore), V (virtual full bore) or F (full bore)\n\nRecommendation: R 49-1 (2013) \"Water meters intended for the metering of cold potable water and hot water\"\n- canonical id: R49 (with part? R49-1). The schema says id canonical e.g. \"R60\". Edition 2013? R 49-1 (2013) — edition might be 2013 with part 1. I'll use id \"R49\", edition 2013, and note part... The schema doesn't have \"part\". Hmm. Maybe id: \"R49-1\"? The rule says canonical e.g. \"R60\" not \"OIML R 60\". R 49-1 is the part 1. I could use id \"R49\" and add part? Not in schema. I'll use \"R49\" as id and edition 2013. Actually R49-1 is a distinct document (Part 1: Metrological and technical requirements). To be safe I'll use \"R49\" with edition 2013, maybe include amendment null omitted. Accuracy classes: 1 and 2 → [\"1\", \"2\"].\n\nTest reports:\n- NMi-2254799-01 dated 14 August 2020, 235 pages\n- NMi-2254799-03 dated 30 September 2020, 44 pages\n- NMi-2254799-04 dated 24 December 2020, 180 pages\n- NMi-2564429-01 dated 2 August 2021, 26 pages\n- NMi-2496280-01 dated 26 August 2021, 29 pages\n- NMi-2496280-02 dated 23 November 2021, 25 pages\n- NMi-2563125-01 dated 22 September 2023, 94 pages\n\nThese are \"results of tests and examinations provided in the associated reports\" → type_evaluation_report role probably. The cert says \"represented by the sample(s) identified in the OIML Type Evaluation Report\". I'll mark them as type_evaluation_report.\n\nDocumentation folder no. T11851-11 → documentation_file.\n\nRevision history: revision \"14\", date? Not given explicitly except the issue date 8 January 2026. The number line says \"revision 14\". Date of certificate: 8 January 2026. I could add revision_history entry with revision \"14\", date \"2026-01-08\", changes null. Hmm, don't fabricate — the revision date isn't stated separately, but the cert is dated 8 January 2026 and is revision 14. It's reasonably implied. I'll include with changes omitted or null. Actually rule 8 says don't fabricate. The date 8 January 2026 is the certificate date; associating it with revision 14 is an inference. I'll include it as it's the current revision's issuance. I'll set changes to null (omit).\n\nCharacteristics — tables 1 and 2 given. Table 1 is for FEW41Y.Z and FEW43Y.Z (X=1 standard, X=3 advanced). Table 2 for FEW45Y.Z (X=5 premium). These differ slightly (power supply, EM environment).\n\nModel family: The cert covers many variants. Models could be enumerated: transmitter types FEW41Y.Z, FEW43Y.Z, FEW45Y.Z and FET4 sensor-only. Given the wildcard structure, I'll define models based on transmitter type: FEW41Y.Z, FEW43Y.Z, FEW45Y.Z. FET4 is the sensor-only variant.\n\nHow to structure? The model_level characteristics vary between Table 1 (FEW41/43) and Table 2 (FEW45). Differing attributes:\n- electromagnetic_environment: E2 (Table 1) vs E1 (with external antenna cable) / E2 (without) for Table 2.\n- power supply details differ.\n\nAlso there are configuration axes: reduced bore vs (virtual) full bore; AC powered vs battery/renewable. Pressure loss class varies by size and bore type → config_level.\n\nLet me structure:\n\ncertificate:\n- number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\"\n- scheme: \"A\"\n- project_number: \"4009997\"\n- page_total: 13\n- member_state: \"The Netherlands\"\n- date_issued: \"2026-01-08\"\n- oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\"\n\nissuing_authority:\n- name: \"NMi Certin B.V., OIML Issuing Authority NL1\" — or name \"NMi Certin B.V.\" with oiml_issuer_id NL1. I'll do name \"NMi Certin B.V.\" and oiml_issuer_id \"NL1\".\n- address_lines: [\"Thijsseweg 11\", \"2629 JA Delft\", \"The Netherlands\"]\n- person_responsible: \"M.Ph.D. Schmidt\"\n- phone: \"+31 88 636 2332\"\n- email: \"certin@nmi.nl\"\n- website: \"www.nmi.nl\"\n- oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\"\n\napplicants: [{name: \"ABB Limited\", address_lines: [\"Oldends Lane\", \"GL10 3TA Stonehouse\", \"United Kingdom\"]}]\n\nmanufacturers: same (Applicant and Manufacturer).\n\ncertified_type:\n- category: \"Water meter\"\n- type_designations: [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"AquaMaster4 FET4\", \"FET4\"] — The type line: \"Type: AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4 *\". So type_designations: [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"AquaMaster4 FET4\"]. Maybe include expansion FEW41Y.Z, FEW43Y.Z, FEW45Y.Z? The tables name FEW41Y.Z and FEW43Y.Z and FEW45Y.Z. I'll include those as they are explicitly mentioned: type_designations: [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"AquaMaster4 FET4\", \"FEW41Y.Z\", \"FEW43Y.Z\", \"FEW45Y.Z\"]. Hmm, keep it reasonable. The wildcard definitions: X ∈ {1,3,5}, Y ∈ {1,2,8,9}, Z ∈ {R,V,F}.\n\n- module_designation: null? Not applicable concept for water meters; omit or null. I'll omit.\n- description: \"An electromagnetic water meter\" plus variant explanation.\n\nmodel_family:\n- family_name: \"AquaMaster4\"\n- models: The cert covers transmitter types FEW41Y.Z, FEW43Y.Z, FEW45Y.Z, and sensor FET4. Also bore types R/V/F and Y variants. Full expansion would be huge (3 X × 4 Y × 3 Z). The tables organize by transmitter type. I'll create models keyed on transmitter group: \"FEW41Y.Z\", \"FEW43Y.Z\", \"FEW45Y.Z\", plus maybe \"FET4\" sensor only. I'll use those as model_id since the characteristics tables are keyed on them.\n\ncharacteristics:\n- type_level: attributes common to all tables:\n  - measuring_principle: \"Electromagnetic\"\n  - accuracy_class: \"1 and 2\" — hmm, accuracy class is in recommendation.accuracy_classes. Also in tables. I could include accuracy_classes: [\"1\",\"2\"] in type_level? The value shape requires structured object: {value, unit_symbol, unit_id, footnote_markers}. value could be \"1 and 2\" string or list. I'll leave accuracy classes to recommendation section and also include in type_level as value \"1 and 2\"? Better: include it once. I'll include in type_level: {\"value\": \"1 and 2\"}? The rule says numbers not strings but this is a class label, string fine.\n  - environmental_class: \"O (installed outdoors)\"\n  - mechanical_class: \"M1\"\n  - temperature_range_ambient: {min: -25, max: 55}, unit °C\n  - water_temperature_class: \"T50\" with range +0.1/+50 °C. I'll record water_temperature_class: {value: \"T50\", ...} and maybe water_temperature_range: {min: 0.1, max: 50} unit °C.\n  - maximum_admissible_pressure: 1.6 MPa (also 16 bar). Record map: value 1.6, unit \"MPa\". Decimal comma → 1.6.\n  - orientation: \"All positions (Horizontal, vertical, or diagonal)\"\n  - reverse_flow: \"The sensor is intended to measure reverse flow\"\n  - checking_facilities: \"Type P (permanent) automatic checking facility\"\n\n- model_level: electromagnetic_environment differs:\n  - FEW41Y.Z: E2\n  - FEW43Y.Z: E2\n  - FEW45Y.Z: \"E1 (with external antenna cable) / E2 (without external antenna cable)\"\n\n- config_level: \n  - flow_profile_sensitivity_class by axis \"bore size / full bore\": U0 and D0 except full bore DN250+ → U3 and D0.\n  - pressure_loss_class by axis \"size and bore type\":\n    - reduced bore, DN40–DN50: Δp 40 (0.40 bar)\n    - reduced bore, >DN50: Δp 63 (0.63 bar)\n    - full bore, DN40–DN50: Δp 10 (0.10 bar)\n    - full bore, DN65–DN200: Δp 16 (0.16 bar)\n    - full bore, DN250+: Δp 10 (0.10 bar)\n\nThese apply to both tables 1 & 2 identically.\n\nPower supply: differs between tables and among three supply options. Config axis \"power supply option\":\nTable 1 (FEW41/43):\n- AC mains: 95–240 VAC @ 50/60 Hz\n- Renewable energy (DC): 6–32 V\n- Replaceable battery: 2x 3.6 V (operating range 2.9–3.6 V)\nTable 2 (FEW45):\n- AC mains: 95–240 VAC @ 50/60 Hz including 4x 3.6 V replaceable batteries\n- Renewable: 6–32 V incl 4x 3.6 V batteries\n- Battery: 6x 3.6 V (2.9–3.6 V)\n\nThis is complex. I could record config_level entries:\n\nattribute \"power_supply_voltage_ac_mains\", axis \"power_supply_option=AC mains\": value {min 95, max 240} V — common to both models.\nattribute \"power_supply_frequency\", axis \"power_supply_option=AC mains\": {min 50, max 60} Hz.\nattribute \"power_supply_voltage_dc\", axis \"power_supply_option=renewable energy (DC)\": {min 6, max 32} V.\nattribute \"battery_configuration\", axis \"model & power_supply_option=battery\": \n- FEW41/43 battery: \"2x 3.6 V\"\n- FEW45 battery: \"6x 3.6 V\"\nThat's model-varying, so maybe model_level: battery_cell_count. Hmm.\n\nSimplest: put power supply in config_level with axis \"power supply option\", with conditions including model qualifier where needed:\n\nconfig_level entries:\n1. attribute: \"power_supply_voltage\", axis: \"power_supply_option\", values: [{condition: \"AC mains\", value: {min:95, max:240}, unit_symbol: \"V\"}, {condition: \"renewable energy / DC powered\", value: {min:6, max:32}, unit_symbol: \"V\"}, {condition: \"replaceable battery (FEW41/FEW43)\", value: 3.6, unit_symbol: \"V\"}...]. This gets messy.\n\nMaybe better: split into multiple config_level attributes:\n- power_supply_voltage_ac: axis \"power_supply_option=AC mains\": value 95–240 V. condition \"AC mains\".\n- power_supply_frequency_ac: 50–60 Hz.\n- power_supply_voltage_dc_renewable: 6–32 V.\n- battery_cells: model dependent → model_level: battery_pack cells? \"2x 3.6 V\" vs \"6x 3.6 V\".\n\nI'll do:\nmodel_level:\n- attribute: \"replaceable_battery_configuration\", values: [{model: \"FEW41Y.Z\", value: \"2x 3.6 V\"}, {model: \"FEW43Y.Z\", value: \"2x 3.6 V\"}, {model: \"FEW45Y.Z\", value: \"6x 3.6 V\"}]. And FEW45 also has 4x 3.6 V backup in AC/DC options.\n\nGiven complexity, maybe keep power supply mostly at config_level with axis \"power supply option\" and note the differences via model-scoped conditions.\n\nLet me draft:\n\nconfig_level: [\n  {\n    \"attribute\": \"power_supply_voltage_ac_mains\",\n    \"axis\": \"power supply option\",\n    \"values\": [{\"condition\": \"AC mains\", \"value\": {\"min\": 95, \"max\": 240}, \"unit_symbol\": \"V\"}]\n  },\n  {\n    \"attribute\": \"power_supply_frequency\",\n    \"axis\": \"power supply option\",\n    \"values\": [{\"condition\": \"AC mains\", \"value\": {\"min\": 50, \"max\": 60}, \"unit_symbol\": \"Hz\"}]\n  },\n  {\n    \"attribute\": \"power_supply_voltage\",\n    \"axis\": \"power supply option\",\n    \"values\": [\n      {\"condition\": \"renewable energy power (DC powered)\", \"value\": {\"min\": 6, \"max\": 32}, \"unit_symbol\": \"V\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"replaceable battery, models FEW41Y.Z / FEW43Y.Z (2 cells)\", \"value\": 3.6, \"unit_symbol\": \"V\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"replaceable battery, model FEW45Y.Z (6 cells)\", \"value\": 3.6, \"unit_symbol\": \"V\"}\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"attribute\": \"battery_operating_range\",\n    ...\n  },\n  {\n    \"attribute\": \"backup_batteries_included\",\n    \"axis\": \"model FEW45Y.Z\",\n    \"values\": [{\"condition\": \"AC mains or renewable energy power\", \"value\": \"4x 3.6 V replaceable batteries\"}]\n  }\n]\n\nHmm, this is getting complicated. The battery operating range 2.9–3.6 V common to both.\n\nMaybe simplify: battery_voltage 3.6 V, battery_quantity model-level (2 vs 6), battery_operating_range 2.9–3.6 V.\n\nLet me settle on a clean structure:\n\nmodel_level entries:\n- electromagnetic_environment\n- replaceable_battery_quantity (2 vs 6)\n\nconfig_level entries:\n- power supply: axis \"power supply option\":\n  - condition \"AC mains\": voltage 95–240 V AC, frequency 50/60 Hz\n  - condition \"renewable energy (DC powered)\": 6–32 V DC\n  - condition \"replaceable battery\": 3.6 V cells, operating range 2.9–3.6 V\n\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: axis \"installation / bore size\":\n  - condition \"sizes up to DN200 (full bore) / all reduced bore sizes\": \"U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream)\"\n  - condition \"full bore sizes DN250 and larger\": \"U3 and D0 (3 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream)\"\n\nActually the condition: default is U0/D0 except full bore DN250+. Conditions: \"all sizes except full bore DN250 and larger\" and \"full bore sizes DN250 and larger\".\n\n- pressure_loss_class: axis \"bore type and size\":\n  - condition \"reduced bore, DN40–DN50\": class Δp40, 0.40 bar\n  - condition \"reduced bore, larger than DN50\": Δp63, 0.63 bar\n  - condition \"(virtual) full bore, DN40–DN50\": Δp10, 0.10 bar\n  - condition \"(virtual) full bore, DN65–DN200\": Δp16, 0.16 bar\n  - condition \"(virtual) full bore, DN250 and larger\": Δp10, 0.10 bar\n\n- temperature_range_ambient common: type_level.\n- water temperature class: \"T50\", range +0.1 to +50 °C → type_level.\n\nmatrix_tables: The fragment doesn't contain actual 2-D data tables beyond the characteristics tables (which are label-value pairs). Tables 1 and 2 are label/value tables. Should I record them as matrix_tables? The schema says matrix_tables preserve multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere. Tables 1 and 2 fit into type/model/config level. I don't think we need matrix_tables. However, I might name table 1 and table 2 as sources. I could optionally include matrix_tables with names but the instruction says include only if needed. I'll skip matrix_tables.\n\nComponents: The water meter consists of flow sensor and flow transmitter/indicating device. Tables 5-12 mention \"family of instruments\" and \"indicating device\". The fragment mentions \"for both flow sensor and flow transmitter\". Components: flow sensor (FET4 sensor only variant), flow transmitter (FEW4...). Hmm — actually FEW4XY.Z are transmitter types per \"General characteristics of AquaMaster4 with transmitter types FEW41Y.Z and FEW43Y.Z\". And FET4 appears to be... \"Y being 1, 2, 8 or 9 (integral/remote version, optionally sensor only)\". The type is \"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4 *\". So the certified type includes flow sensor FET4 and transmitters FEW4XY.Z. Let me add components:\n- role: \"flow sensor\", type_designations: [\"FET4\"]\n- role: \"flow transmitter\", type_designations: [\"FEW41Y.Z\", \"FEW43Y.Z\", \"FEW45Y.Z\"] (wildcards FEW4XY.Z)\n- role: \"indicating device\" — mentioned in tables 9-12 list. Yes \"General characteristics of the indicating device...\" — the transmitter serves as indicating device presumably. I'll include transmitter only, and maybe note indicating device. I'll include \"indicating device\" role with null designations? Don't fabricate — indicating device is mentioned but no type designation. I'll skip it or include with empty type_designations. I'll skip.\n\nComponents with alternatives: FEW41Y.Z OR FEW43Y.Z OR FEW45Y.Z → alternatives \"OR\".\n\nAlso sensor-only option mentioned. Keep components simple: flow sensor FET4, flow transmitter FEW4XY.Z (variants FEW41/43/45).\n\nrecommendation:\n- id: \"R49\" — but it's R 49-1. I'll use id \"R49-1\"? The instruction: canonical e.g. \"R60\" not \"OIML R 60\". For parts, I think \"R49\" with edition 2013 and maybe note part in... there's no part field. Many extraction schemas would use \"R49-1\". Hmm. The example R60 example didn't have parts. I'll use \"R49\" as id and edition 2013 — but that loses part info. Actually since the certificate specifically references R 49-1 (2013), and R49 has parts 1 and 2, the most faithful canonical id might be \"R49-1\". But rule says canonical like \"R60\". I'll go with \"R49\" and edition 2013, and I could add \"part\": 1? Not in schema — omit extra fields? Adding extra fields may be acceptable but risky. I'll use id \"R49-1\" — hmm.\n\nLet me re-read: \"recommendation: id (canonical, e.g. \"R60\" not \"OIML R 60\"), edition (int), amendment, scheme, accuracy_classes[]\". I think \"R49\" is the canonical recommendation id, edition 2013. Part 1 vs 2 is a subtlety. I'll use \"R49\" and include edition 2013. Hmm, but strictly the recommendation document is R 49-1. I'll go with \"R49-1\"? \n\nConsidering downstream use (matching recommendations), \"R49\" is the OIML recommendation number; \"-1\" is part. I'll use \"R49\" for id and add the part info in... nothing. Actually, I could put id \"R49-1\" since that's the precise reference. The instruction's example strips \"OIML\" prefix only. I'll choose \"R49-1\" as it preserves the exact reference without \"OIML\" prefix. Hmm, but edition: R 49-1 (2013) — edition 2013. Wait, but the certificate number is R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01. OK.\n\nI'll go with id \"R49\", edition 2013. Both defensible. Let me think about which is more useful: The system likely deduplicates by recommendation id; R49-1 would create a separate entry from R49-2 certs. That's arguably correct since they're separate documents with separate requirements. I'll use \"R49-1\". Hmm, actually, wait — the instruction says 'id (canonical, e.g. \"R60\" not \"OIML R 60\")'. The canonicalization shown is removing the \"OIML\" prefix. For part-numbered recommendations like R 49-1, the full designation includes the part. I'll keep \"R49-1\".\n\n- edition: 2013\n- scheme: \"A\"\n- accuracy_classes: [\"1\", \"2\"]\n\ntest_reports: 7 entries, role \"type_evaluation_report\". Documentation folder T11851-11 → documentation_file. The text: \"The construction of the measuring instrument is recorded in the Documentation folder no. T11851-11.\" Add to test_reports with role documentation_file, id \"T11851-11\", no date/pages.\n\nrevision_history: [{revision: \"14\", date: \"2026-01-08\"}] — changes not stated; I'll omit \"changes\" or set null. Rule: revision as string \"14\".\n\nfootnotes: The \"*\" footnote about X/Y/Z meanings — it's marked with \"*\" in the type designation. Extract as footnote: marker \"*\", text: \"With X being 1 for standard or 3 for advanced or 5 for premium (mobile Comms - MC) variant; Y being 1, 2, 8 or 9 (integral/remote version, optionally sensor only); Z being R (reduced bore), V (virtual full bore) or F (full bore).\" This is a definitional note. I'll include it in footnotes.\n\nNow, date_issued: 8 January 2026 → \"2026-01-08\".\n\npage_total: 13.\n\nNow model_family models: which granularity? Options:\na) Expand full wildcards: 3 X × 4 Y × 3 Z = 36 combos for FEW4 + FET4 with same wildcards — too many and speculative.\nb) Use transmitter groups as models: FEW41Y.Z, FEW43Y.Z, FEW45Y.Z, FET4.\n\nI'll go with (b): models: [{\"model_id\": \"FEW41Y.Z\"}, {\"model_id\": \"FEW43Y.Z\"}, {\"model_id\": \"FEW45Y.Z\"}, {\"model_id\": \"FET4\"}]. Hmm, but FEW4XY.Z is a family pattern; FEW41Y.Z etc. are the actual groups referenced in tables. FET4 is the sensor type. Actually \"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4 *\" — FET4 presumably also has wildcard? \"FET4 *\" with the same footnote? The footnote describes X, Y, Z for the FEW types. FET4* — the asterisk footnote explains X, Y, Z. FET4 likely is the flow sensor designation (sensor only variant). I'll include FET4 as a model too.\n\nActually wait: \"Type: AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4 *\" — maybe the sensor is FET4 and transmitter FEW4XY.Z. Tables speak of \"AquaMaster4 with transmitter types FEW41Y.Z and FEW43Y.Z\". So transmitters FEW4..., sensor FET4. Components: flow sensor FET4; flow transmitter FEW41Y.Z/43/45.\n\nFor model_family, models = transmitter variants: FEW41Y.Z, FEW43Y.Z, FEW45Y.Z. Plus maybe \"FET4 (sensor only)\". I'll include 4 models: FEW41Y.Z, FEW43Y.Z, FEW45Y.Z, FET4.\n\nAttributes in model_level:\n- electromagnetic_environment: FEW41Y.Z: E2; FEW43Y.Z: E2; FEW45Y.Z: \"E1 (with external antenna cable) / E2 (without)\". \n\n- replaceable_battery_configuration: FEW41: \"2x 3.6 V\"; FEW43: \"2x 3.6 V\"; FEW45: \"6x 3.6 V\". And FEW45 AC/DC options include 4x 3.6 V replaceable batteries. This is a string value.\n\nAlso power supply voltage ranges are common across models → config_level axis \"power supply option\".\n\nStructured value shape: each value must be object {value, unit_symbol, unit_id, footnote_markers}. For model_level, \"values\" entries: {model, value, footnote_markers} — the R137 example shows {\"model\": \"G1.6\", \"value\": 2.5} without unit_symbol per entry (unit at attribute level). For config_level: \"{condition, value, unit_symbol}\". OK.\n\nLet me now also handle \"Maximum admissible pressure (MAP)\": value 1.6 MPa; parenthetical 16 bar. I'll record map: {value: 1.6, unit_symbol: \"MPa\"} and maybe map_bar: 16 bar? Or just one. I'll include maximum_admissible_pressure with value 1.6 MPa and a secondary? Keep single: value 1.6, unit \"MPa\". Maybe add note? Could include both via two characteristics: maximum_admissible_pressure (MPa) and maximum_admissible_pressure (bar)? Slightly redundant but informative. I'll include just MPa to keep clean. Hmm, the 16 bar is useful. I'll add \"maximum_admissible_pressure\" {1.6 MPa} only.\n\nWater temperature class T50 (+0.1 °C / +50 °C): water_temperature_class value \"T50\"; water_temperature_range {min: 0.1, max: 50} °C.\n\nTemperature range ambient: {min: -25, max: 55} °C.\n\nAccuracy class: \"1 and 2\" — string value.\n\nEnvironmental class: \"O (installed outdoors)\" — string. Or value \"O\" with description. I'll use value \"O (installed outdoors)\".\n\nElectromagnetic environment: model_level as discussed. Actually, for models FEW41/FEW43 it's E2; for FEW45 E1 with external antenna cable else E2. Values as strings.\n\nMechanical class: \"M1\".\n\nMeasuring principle: \"Electromagnetic\".\n\nOrientation: \"All positions (horizontal, vertical, or diagonal)\".\n\nReverse flow: \"The sensor is intended to measure reverse flow\" → maybe boolean-ish string. Keep string.\n\nChecking facilities: \"Type P (permanent) automatic checking facility\".\n\nSoftware identification: \"See details in table 3/4\" — omit or include as string pointing to tables. Omit; tables 3/4 content not in fragment.\n\nFlow profile sensitivity: config_level axis \"bore size / installation\".\n\nPressure loss class: config_level with axis \"bore type and size\", values with condition strings and value objects {value: class \"Δp40\", unit?} — The value has class Δp40 and 0.40 bar. I could record pressure_loss as numeric bar with condition including class: condition \"reduced bore, DN40–DN50 (class Δp 40)\", value 0.40 bar. Good.\n\nNow build JSON.\n\ncertificate: {\n  \"number\": \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\",\n  \"scheme\": \"A\",\n  \"project_number\": \"4009997\",\n  \"page_total\": 13,\n  \"member_state\": \"The Netherlands\",\n  \"date_issued\": \"2026-01-08\",\n  \"oiml_issuer_id\": \"NL1\"\n}\n\nissuing_authority: {\n  \"name\": \"NMi Certin B.V.\",\n  \"address_lines\": [\"Thijssseweg 11\", \"2629 JA Delft\", \"The Netherlands\"],\n  \"person_responsible\": \"M.Ph.D. Schmidt\",\n  \"phone\": \"+31 88 636 2332\",\n  \"email\": \"certin@nmi.nl\",\n  \"website\": \"www.nmi.nl\",\n  \"oiml_issuer_id\": \"NL1\"\n}\n\nNote markdown spells \"Thijssseweg 11\" (typo with double s). Actual address is Thijsseweg 11. I'll transcribe as given: \"Thijssseweg 11\". Keep as-is.\n\napplicants: [{name \"ABB Limited\", address_lines [\"Oldends Lane\", \"GL10 3TA Stonehouse\", \"United Kingdom\"]}]\n\nmanufacturers: same.\n\ncertified_type: {\n  \"category\": \"Water meter\",\n  \"type_designations\": [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"AquaMaster4 FET4\", \"FEW41Y.Z\", \"FEW43Y.Z\", \"FEW45Y.Z\"],\n  \"description\": \"An electromagnetic water meter. AquaMaster4 with transmitter types FEW41Y.Z, FEW43Y.Z and 